## DESCRIPTION
## A dynamic plot of a quadratic function
## ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(WeBWorK)
## DBchapter(WeBWorK tutorial)
## DBsection(PGML tutorial 2015)
## Date(07/01/2025)
## Institution(Boise State University)
## Author(Jaimos Skriletz)
## MO(1)
## KEYWORDS('algebra', 'precalculus', 'quadratic', 'plot')
#:% name = Function Plot
#:% type = Sample
#:% subject = [algebra, precalculus, plot]
#:% categories = [plots]
#:% section = preamble
DOCUMENT()
#:% section = setup
#: For this problem, a plot of $y = ax^2$ for a random value of $a$ is
#: generated. Create a Plot object with the given plotting window and other
#: axis properties.
#:
#: The `add_function` method takes a string with the function and properties of
#: the plot. The `{-3,3}` is the domain of the plot where curly braces produces
#: arrows.  Other formats for this are `(-3,3)` for open endpoints and `[-3,3]`
#: are closed endpoints.  They can be mixed.
a = non_zero_random((-3), 3)
plot = Plot( xmin = -3.2, xmax = 3.2, ymin = -10, ymax = 10, xtick_delta = 1, ytick_delta = 2, xlabel = '\\(x\\)', ylabel = '\\(y\\)', )
plot.add_function(f"{a} x^2 for x in {{-3,3}} using color:blue, width:3")
#:% section = statement
#: By default, the plot uses JSXGraph to plot the graph in HTML and will use
#: tikz/pgfplots to produce the hardcopy.
#:
#: The `[! !]{}{}` is a PGML format for inserting an image, with the first
#: argument is the alternate text for the image, the second argument is the
#: plot and the third is the width of the plot.
pgml_block_0 = '''
The following plot is a quadratic:

>>[! Plot of a quadratic function !]{plot}{500}<<

What is the form of the function?  [`f(x)=`] [_]{Compute("a x^2")}

'''
TEXT(PGML(pgml_block_0))
#:% section = solution
pgml_block_1 = '''
Solution explanation goes here.
'''
SOLUTION(PGML(pgml_block_1))
ENDDOCUMENT()